Candidate types (names provisional, semantics frozen before implementation):
interface ByteSink {
  write(chunk: Uint8Array): Promise<void>;
  readonly ownedOutput?: {
    readonly consumerClosed: AbortSignal;
    write(chunk: Uint8Array): Promise<void>;
  };
}
interface OutputOperation {
  readonly signal: AbortSignal;
  readonly output: ByteSink;
  registerCleanup(cleanup: InvocationCleanup): void;
  acquire<T>(start: (signal: AbortSignal) => T | Promise<T>,
             release: (resource: T) => void | Promise<void>): Promise<T>;
  close(): Promise<void>;
}
createOutputOperation(context: Pick<CommandContext, 'signal' | 'registerCleanup'>,
                      destination: ByteSink): OutputOperation

Sink-bound capability chosen because actual destinations survive descriptor
redirection and context.invoke. Transparent budget/signal wrappers must preserve
both close provenance and accounted owned writes. The owned write path must NOT
mark legacy stage writes or trigger stage-wide EPIPE cancellation. Opaque sinks
without the optional capability support caller cancellation/cleanup only: no
fabricated reader-demand knowledge. Writable completion/empty success is NOT
consumer close. BytePipe consumer return/abort supplies the actual capability.

Lifecycle: construct -> synchronously register idempotent close with existing
07acb invocation cleanup -> enroll listeners -> OPEN/admission -> start owned IO
immediately (no demand gate). Consumer close -> first operation reason EPIPE ->
CLOSING/admission shut -> abort operation signal only -> drain registered
cooperative releases -> CLOSED. Normal close drains without manufacturing EPIPE.
Register cleanup before acquisition; finally awaits the same close promise.
Late opaque acquisition is never awaited by close; fulfillment after admission
closes is refused and released, rejection observed. Only registered cooperative
cleanup is awaited. Host callbacks must honor this cooperation contract.

Reason precedence: first operation abort reason wins identity; already-aborted
caller wins enrollment races. Later caller abort still wins public exec outcome
through existing caller signal even if operation reason remains EPIPE. Explicit
command failures stay failures, not EPIPE unless that operation actually aborted;
cleanup failures are observed and surfaced when no earlier operation error wins.
EPIPE maps to existing 141 producer status, normal pipefail/rightmost rules stay.
Commands may catch operation EPIPE and continue independent work; no stage abort.

Ownership matrix / intended bounded integrations:
- explicit custom source factory -> operation signal, owned iterator release;
- cat named VFS input -> own per-destination work before stat/open/read;
- borrowed stdin -> NEVER enroll or return/cancel on mere stdout closure;
- curl stdout-only transfer -> enroll before authorization/request (preheaders);
- curl any necessary -o/-O body file or -D header file -> request remains under
  caller/transfer lifetime, not stdout lifetime; enroll stdout writes separately;
  a closed stdout must not prevent independent body/header file completion;
- stderr diagnostics remain caller-owned; -w stdout is its own output operation;
- no default automatic enrollment of other commands, no global stage shortcut.

Acceptance intentions (not claimed results): unchanged original5 baseline replay
must retain 1200ms failures. Adapted5 keep exact starts/barriers/deadlines/commands
and require ONE started read/request, zero first writes, EPIPE operation reason,
stage/caller NOT globally aborted, cleanup before fixture teardown. Publish exact
binding/assertion delta separately; never call adapted5 unchanged original5green.
Replay 57+9 controls and pinned GNU5.3 effects; C9 remains synthetic/non-product.
Author regressions <=12 logical cases cover independent file/stderr/empty/error,
borrowed retention, nested destinations, actual curl mixed outputs/preheaders/body,
caller precedence, late acquisition/rejection, exact-once cooperative cleanup.
No production permission; no superiority/release/full-gate claim.

Pre-implementation addendum v1.1: actual Node transport needs optional
HttpRequest.registerCleanup?: (cleanup: InvocationCleanup) => void, forwarding
the existing 07acb hook. It registers idempotent request/socket close BEFORE
http.request, including pre-response cancellation. Custom opaque transports may
omit cooperative registration; late acquired responses are disposed/observed,
never awaited as opaque acquisitions. This is a prototype type addition, not an
assertion about the current released HttpRequest interface.

